\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}

\paragraph{Model and Predictor.}
We evaluate on LLaMA-3.1-8B~\cite{grattafiori2024llama3}, which uses SwiGLU FFN blocks~\cite{shazeer2020glu} with $d_{\text{model}}=4096$ and $d_{\text{ff}}=14336$ across 32 layers.
The sparsity predictor is a two-layer MLP with 2.37M parameters per layer (75.9M total), implemented as Linear($4096 \to 128$) + GELU + Linear($128 \to 14336$), trained to produce per-neuron importance scores that minimize output-distribution divergence under sparsity.

\paragraph{Training.}
All predictors are trained for 1000 steps on WikiText-103 with sequence length 2048 and batch size 4 on a single NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell Server Edition GPU (96\,GB), using AdamW~\cite{loshchilov2019adamw} with learning rate $1 \times 10^{-3}$, weight decay $5 \times 10^{-2}$, and cosine decay.
The KL predictor uses Gumbel-Sigmoid relaxation~\cite{jang2017gumbel} with temperature $\tau=1.0$ annealed to $0.1$ over training.

\paragraph{Evaluation.}
We report perplexity on WikiText-2~\cite{merity2017pointer} (seq\_len=2048) and downstream accuracy on ARC-Challenge (25-shot), WinoGrande (5-shot), MMLU (5-shot), and GSM8K (8-shot).

\paragraph{Inference Protocol.}
At inference, the predictor produces per-neuron scores that are passed to TEAL's global top-$k$ allocation mechanism~\cite{liu2025teal}, which distributes the sparsity budget across layers non-uniformly.
We term this \textit{predictor-TEAL}: the predictor replaces TEAL's magnitude-based scoring while retaining its allocation strategy.

\paragraph{Baselines.}
We compare against: (1) \textbf{Vanilla TEAL}~\cite{liu2025teal}, which uses activation magnitudes directly; (2) \textbf{BCE predictor} (DejaVu-style~\cite{liu2023dejavu}), trained with per-neuron binary cross-entropy on magnitude-based oracle masks for 1000 and 2700 steps; (3) \textbf{Uniform allocation}, which distributes the sparsity budget equally across layers; and (4) \textbf{WINA}~\cite{liu2025teal}, an importance-weighted variant.


\subsection{Main Results}

Table~\ref{tab:main} presents the primary comparison. Uniform allocation preserves downstream performance best; global allocation is analyzed to understand allocation-quality tradeoffs (\S\ref{sec:scaling}).
At 30\% sparsity, KL distillation with global TEAL allocation achieves PPL 8.96$\pm$0.02 (3-seed) and reduces TEAL's degradation from dense (10.97 $\to$ 8.96, an 18\% improvement). % exp_id: kl30_3seed_global
HellaSwag (86.0\% retention) and PIQA (92.5\% retention) confirm moderate degradation on non-reasoning tasks (Appendix~\ref{app:downstream}). % exp_id: hellaswag_piqa_kl30_uniform
Uniform allocation at 30\% achieves PPL 8.81$\pm$0.02 with stable downstream performance (MMLU 40.7$\pm$1.3\%, 3-seed), while global top-$k$ exhibits high seed variance (35.7$\pm$9.5\%) from late-layer starvation (L31 retention 28.4\%) interacting with predictor initialization (\S\ref{sec:scaling}). % exp_id: bench_kl30_uniform, kl30_s123_uniform, kl30_s456_uniform, kl30_3seed

At 50\% sparsity, KL distillation over vanilla TEAL widens the PPL advantage: 11.79 vs.\ 23.31, a 49\% reduction. % exp_id: bench_kl50_global, bench_teal50
The predictor generalizes to C4 validation at both 30\% (PPL 16.03$\pm$0.01, 2-seed) and 50\% (52.5\% over TEAL; Appendix~\ref{app:ood}).
However, all methods exhibit downstream collapse on MMLU (28.1$\pm$0.2\% vs.\ 28.0$\pm$0.6\%, both $\approx$ random, 3-seed) and GSM8K ($<$2.2\%); this is a capacity-limited regime where no allocation strategy can recover. % exp_id: bench_kl50_global, d030_kl50_uniform, multi_seed_kl50_global

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Main results on LLaMA-3.1-8B. PPL$\downarrow$ on WikiText-2; accuracy$\uparrow$ on downstream tasks. \textbf{Bold}: best among compensation-free methods per sparsity level. $^\ast$BCE at 50\% follows the DejaVu~\cite{liu2023dejavu} pipeline with FastForward-style~\cite{gautam2026fastforward} compensation (67.2M params), no TEAL allocation. $^\dagger$BCE at 30\%: TEAL global allocation, no compensation (2700 steps). Entries with $\pm$ report 3-seed mean$\pm$std ($s \in \{42, 123, 456\}$); 50\% KL rows report seed 42 (3-seed stability in Appendix~\ref{app:stability}: PPL CV$<$1\%, MMLU $\sigma \leq$0.6pp); remaining single-seed entries use seed 42.}
\label{tab:main}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{
\begin{tabular}{llccccc}
\toprule
Sparsity & Method & PPL$\downarrow$ & ARC-c & WG & MMLU & GSM8K \\
\midrule
0\% & Dense & 6.12 & 58.28 & 78.06 & 65.27 & 48.75 \\
\midrule
\multirow{4}{*}{30\%}
& TEAL vanilla & 10.97 & 42.32 & 66.06 & 38.61 & 7.88 \\ % exp_id: bench_teal30
& KL global (Ours) & 8.96$\pm$0.02 & \textbf{43.8$\pm$3.6} & \textbf{70.2$\pm$0.9} & 35.7$\pm$9.5 & \textbf{8.0$\pm$0.7} \\ % exp_id: kl30_3seed_global
& KL uniform & \textbf{8.81$\pm$0.02} & 43.7$\pm$2.5 & 68.9$\pm$0.4 & \textbf{40.7$\pm$1.3} & 7.5$\pm$0.1 \\ % exp_id: bench_kl30_uniform, kl30_s123_uniform, kl30_s456_uniform
& BCE global$^\dagger$ & 11.25 & 41.2 & 66.0 & 36.3 & 6.0 \\ % exp_id: bce30_teal_global
\midrule
\multirow{4}{*}{50\%}
& TEAL vanilla & 23.31 & 28.41 & 53.04 & \textbf{28.32} & 1.97 \\ % exp_id: bench_teal50
& KL global (Ours) & \textbf{11.79} & \textbf{32.85} & \textbf{63.46} & 28.1 & 1.29 \\ % exp_id: multi_seed_kl50_global
& KL uniform & 11.92 & 32.76 & 62.90 & 28.0 & \textbf{2.12} \\ % exp_id: multi_seed_kl50_uniform
& BCE + comp.$^\ast$ & 28.13$\pm$0.20 & 27.6$\pm$0.6 & 51.8$\pm$0.6 & 26.1$\pm$0.5 & 2.1$\pm$0.2 \\ % exp_id: bce50_3seed
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}


\subsection{Extended Training Analysis}
\label{sec:isocompute}

To test whether BCE simply requires more compute, we train it for 2700 steps (2.71$\times$ the KL step budget, approximately equal wall-clock time: $\sim$46 min BCE vs.\ $\sim$45 min KL on RTX PRO 6000).
The BCE predictor achieves PPL 28.13$\pm$0.20 (3-seed), still 2.39$\times$ worse than KL at 1000 steps (PPL 11.79). % exp_id: bce50_3seed
The difference is systematic, not due to architectural or compute limitations: both predictors use identical architectures and TEAL allocation, yet BCE achieves +7pp oracle F1 (0.613 vs.\ 0.540) with 139\% worse PPL (28.13 vs.\ 11.79). KL's near-random precision (0.51) is consistent with a mask optimized for distributional fidelity rather than magnitude agreement. % exp_id: f1_oracle_analysis, bce_isocompute_s42
Coupled training correlates with better output preservation; the causal ablation in \S\ref{sec:mask_analysis} provides evidence that target quality, not gradient coupling, is the primary driver.


\subsection{Mask Pattern Analysis}
\label{sec:mask_analysis}

To understand \emph{what} the KL predictor learns, we compare KL-derived masks against magnitude-based oracle masks (the BCE training targets) at 50\% sparsity on WikiText-2.
Per-layer Jaccard similarity averages 32\% at 50\% sparsity (64 samples, 131K tokens): the KL predictor discovers neuron importance patterns that diverge 68\% from activation magnitude, with divergence scaling from 46\% at 30\% to 68\% at 50\% (Figure~\ref{fig:mask_heatmap}).
At layer 16, 41\% of neurons fall in disagreement quadrants where KL and magnitude rankings diverge (Appendix~\ref{app:mask_viz}, Figures~\ref{fig:mask_heatmap}--\ref{fig:scatter}).
Despite this divergence, KL achieves PPL 11.79 vs.\ BCE's 28.13$\pm$0.20: output-distribution fidelity, not magnitude agreement, determines mask quality under global allocation. % exp_id: bench_kl50_global, bce50_3seed, c1_mask_overlap

\paragraph{Per-Layer Divergence Structure.}
Per-layer Jaccard distance follows a U-shaped pattern (Figure~\ref{fig:mask_heatmap}): at 50\%, middle layers (L14--L17) show lowest divergence ($\sim$0.61), while late layers (L22--L28) peak at $\sim$0.70--0.75. The same structure holds at 30\% (range 0.361--0.600).
Late layers are closest to the KL-optimized output distribution, so their backward gradient carries stronger output-sensitivity signal and the predictor deviates most from magnitude ranking there. % exp_id: c1_mask_overlap

\paragraph{Causal Ablation: Target Quality vs.\ Gradient Coupling.}
To disentangle whether KL's advantage stems from its coupled gradient pathway or from the mask patterns it discovers, we train a standard BCE predictor on KL-derived oracle masks instead of magnitude-based masks (C1 ablation).
Table~\ref{tab:c1} reports results under TEAL uniform allocation at matched sparsity.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Causal ablation (C1): BCE trained on KL-derived masks vs.\ KL predictor from main table (same seed), TEAL uniform. Max $\Delta$=1.70pp (s42 MMLU) at 30\% (3-seed); 0.59pp at 50\% (2-seed).}
\label{tab:c1}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{
\begin{tabular}{lllccccc}
\toprule
Sparsity & Seed & Method & PPL$\downarrow$ & ARC-c & WG & MMLU & GSM8K \\
\midrule
\multirow{6}{*}{30\%}
& s42 & C1 & 9.45 & 48.63 & 70.40 & \textbf{42.30} & 13.57 \\ % exp_id: c1_ablation_30pct_s42_eval_v2
& & KL & \textbf{8.80} & --- & --- & 40.6 & --- \\ % exp_id: bench_kl30_uniform
& s123 & C1 & 8.84 & 44.71 & \textbf{69.77} & \textbf{39.92} & 6.82 \\ % exp_id: eval_c1_retrain_30pct_s123
& & KL & \textbf{8.83} & --- & --- & 39.4 & --- \\ % exp_id: kl30_s123_uniform_v3
& s456 & C1 & 8.82 & 44.71 & \textbf{69.93} & 41.04 & 6.60 \\ % exp_id: eval_c1_retrain_30pct_s456
& & KL & \textbf{8.79} & --- & --- & \textbf{42.0} & --- \\ % exp_id: kl30_s456_uniform_v3
\midrule
\multirow{2}{*}{50\%}
& s42 & C1 & 11.96 & 32.17 & \textbf{63.14} & 27.79 & \textbf{2.05} \\ % exp_id: c1_bce_kl_targets_teal50u
& & KL & 11.96 & \textbf{32.59} & 62.35 & \textbf{27.92} & 1.97 \\ % exp_id: c1_kl_teal50u
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

At 50\%, C1 matches KL within statistical noise (max $\Delta$=0.59pp, 2-seed), while magnitude-trained BCE yields PPL 28.13$\pm$0.20 ($2.4\times$ worse; Table~\ref{tab:main}).
At 30\%, C1 reproduces KL quality within $\pm$1.7pp on MMLU (max $\Delta$=1.70pp on s42, 3-seed); the larger PPL gap (9.45 vs.\ 8.80) reflects greater per-decision weight at lower sparsity.
These results support target quality, not gradient coupling, as the primary driver. % exp_id: bce50_3seed, c1_bce_kl_targets_30u, c1_s123

\paragraph{Soft-vs-Hard Confound.}
C1 changes both gradient coupling and mask representation (hard binary vs.\ soft Gumbel-Sigmoid). However, BCE with identical Gumbel-Sigmoid relaxation yields PPL 38.02 (Table~\ref{tab:ablation}), worse than standard hard-target BCE (27.97): softness alone does not explain KL's advantage. % exp_id: bce_gumbel_lagrangian_isolation, eval_bce_v3_s42
Disentangling target quality from gradient representation remains for future work.

\subsection{Ablation Studies}

Table~\ref{tab:ablation} isolates the contribution of each design choice at 50\% sparsity.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Ablation at 50\% sparsity on LLaMA-3.1-8B (WikiText-2 PPL$\downarrow$, unified benchmark protocol).}
\label{tab:ablation}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Configuration & PPL$\downarrow$ & $\Delta$ from Ours \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{3}{l}{\textit{KL-based variants}} \\
Forward KL + Gumbel (Ours) & \textbf{11.79} & --- \\ % exp_id: bench_kl50_global
JSD (symmetric) + Gumbel & 12.54 & +0.75 \\ % exp_id: jsd_ablation_s42
Reverse KL + Gumbel & 12.80 & +1.01 \\ % exp_id: reverse_kl_ablation_s42
Forward KL + STE & 15.77 & +3.98 \\ % exp_id: ste_baseline_s42
\midrule
\multicolumn{3}{l}{\textit{BCE-based variants}} \\
BCE + Compensation (hard mask) & 25.44 & +13.65 \\ % exp_id: bce_comp_staged_s42
BCE (no compensation) & 27.97 & +16.18 \\ % exp_id: eval_bce_v3_s42
BCE + Gumbel + Penalty (no KL) & 38.02 & +26.23 \\ % exp_id: bce_gumbel_lagrangian_isolation
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Gumbel vs.\ STE.}
Replacing Gumbel-Sigmoid with STE~\cite{bengio2013ste} degrades PPL from 11.79 to 15.77 (+3.98), which accounts for 34.5\% of the improvement over vanilla TEAL. % exp_id: ste_baseline_s42

\paragraph{Divergence Direction.}
Forward KL (mode-covering) outperforms JSD (+6.4\%) and reverse KL (+8.6\%), consistent with the SNR analysis in \S\ref{sec:method:gradient}: complete teacher supervision is associated with better global allocation quality. % exp_id: jsd_ablation_s42, reverse_kl_ablation_s42
This contrasts with MiniLLM~\cite{gu2024minillm} (reverse KL for generation); in mask prediction, mode-covering ensures no important neuron is omitted.

\paragraph{SPON Contribution.}
Adding SPON~\cite{xu2026spon} bias correction reduces PPL from 12.08 to 11.79 (2.4\% relative; Appendix~\ref{app:spon}).\footnote{SPON analysis uses \texttt{evaluate.py} (per-token); main results use \texttt{benchmark\_eval.py} (TEAL allocation). The 0.29 PPL gap reflects allocation protocol.} % exp_id: spon_2048_s42, mvp_kl_norm_v2_s42

\paragraph{Loss Function Isolation.}
BCE with identical Gumbel-Sigmoid and penalty yields PPL 38.02, worse than vanilla BCE (27.97): the penalty interferes with per-neuron optimization without global coordination. % exp_id: bce_gumbel_lagrangian_isolation
The loss effect ($\Delta$PPL=26.23) is $6.6\times$ the relaxation effect ($\Delta$PPL=3.98).

\paragraph{Compensation Redundancy.}
Adding compensation~\cite{gautam2026fastforward} (+67.2M params) to KL provides no benefit at either tested sparsity: at 50\%, all five metrics are identical to KL-only; at 30\%, PPL 8.97$\pm$0.01 vs.\ 8.96$\pm$0.03 (2-seed). BCE requires compensation (27.97$\to$25.44), yet remains $2.16\times$ worse than KL without it. Training converges by step 700 (Appendix~\ref{app:comp_convergence}). % exp_id: kl_comp_30_s42, kl_comp_30_s456, kl_comp_50_s42, bce_comp_staged_s42

\paragraph{Hyperparameter Robustness.}
Appendix~\ref{app:db_sensitivity} confirms robustness to predictor capacity ($d_b$, $<$3\%), temperature ($<$1.8\%), and training steps (saturated by 800). % exp_id: db_sensitivity_sweep, tau_schedule_sweep, training_steps_sweep


\subsection{Sparsity Scaling and Regime Analysis}
\label{sec:scaling}

Figure~\ref{fig:sparsity_sweep} shows PPL as a function of sparsity for the KL predictor under global allocation.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig2_sparsity_sweep.pdf}
\caption{Perplexity scaling with sparsity. The KL predictor's advantage grows disproportionately as more neurons are pruned.}
\label{fig:sparsity_sweep}
\end{figure}

The regime curve (Figure~\ref{fig:sparsity_sweep}) is strictly monotonic from PPL 8.17 at 25\% to 11.80 at 50\% (global, seed 42), with KL/Dense degradation ranging from $1.34\times$ to $1.93\times$.
At matched points, KL improves over TEAL by 18\% at 30\% and 49\% at 50\%; at 70\%, where TEAL collapses (PPL 100.11), the advantage reaches 82\%. % exp_id: regime_kl25_global, regime_kl50_global, kl_70_global_teal

\paragraph{Allocation Behavior Across Sparsity Levels.}
Table~\ref{tab:phase} compares global vs.\ uniform allocation at representative sparsity levels, revealing qualitative shifts in preference.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Allocation analysis at key sparsity levels (LLaMA-3.1-8B, PPL and MMLU; full 6-level data with all downstream metrics in Appendix~\ref{app:alloc_full}). $\pm$: 3-seed; others: seed 42. $^\ast$High seed variance (25.3--43.5\%).}
\label{tab:phase}
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{llcc}
\toprule
Sparsity & Allocation & PPL$\downarrow$ & MMLU \\
\midrule
\multirow{2}{*}{25\%}
& Global & \textbf{8.17} & \textbf{46.6} \\ % exp_id: regime_kl25_global
& Uniform & 8.23 & 44.0 \\ % exp_id: regime_kl25_uniform
\midrule
\multirow{2}{*}{30\%}
& Global & 8.96$\pm$0.02 & 35.7$\pm$9.5$^\ast$ \\ % exp_id: kl30_3seed_global
& Uniform & \textbf{8.81$\pm$0.02} & \textbf{40.7$\pm$1.3} \\ % exp_id: bench_kl30_uniform
\midrule
\multirow{2}{*}{35\%}
& Global & \textbf{9.31} & \textbf{40.4} \\ % exp_id: regime_kl35_global
& Uniform & 9.44 & 37.8 \\ % exp_id: regime_kl35_uniform
\midrule
\multirow{2}{*}{50\%}
& Global & \textbf{11.79} & \textbf{28.1$\pm$0.2} \\ % exp_id: multi_seed_kl50_global
& Uniform & 11.92$\pm$0.07 & 28.0$\pm$0.6 \\ % exp_id: multi_seed_kl50_uniform
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The analysis reveals \emph{non-monotonic} allocation preference with three regimes (full 6-level data in Appendix~\ref{app:alloc_full}):

\noindent\textbf{Budget-surplus ($\leq$30\%).}
At 25\%, PPL differs by only 0.06. At 30\%, uniform achieves PPL 8.81$\pm$0.02 (3-seed) with $7.5\times$ smaller cross-seed MMLU $\sigma$ (1.3pp vs.\ 9.5pp). % exp_id: regime_kl25_global, regime_kl25_uniform, kl30_3seed

\noindent\textbf{Budget-constrained (35--40\%).}
Global becomes preferable: at 35\%, MMLU 40.4\% vs.\ 37.8\% (+2.6pp); at 40\%, MMLU 38.1\% (Appendix~\ref{app:alloc_full}). % exp_id: regime_kl35_global, regime_kl35_uniform, regime_kl40_global

\noindent\textbf{Capacity-limited ($\geq$45\%).}
Gap vanishes for reasoning: at 50\%, MMLU collapses to near-random under both strategies (28.1 vs.\ 28.0\%), though global still helps non-reasoning tasks (HellaSwag +2.29pp; Appendix~\ref{app:downstream}). Late-layer starvation persists (Figure~\ref{fig:retention}; Appendix~\ref{app:retention}). % exp_id: multi_seed_kl50_global, multi_seed_kl50_uniform, hellaswag_piqa_kl50_global, hellaswag_piqa_kl50_uniform


\paragraph{Cross-Architecture and Scale.}
\label{sec:cross_arch}
On Mistral-7B-v0.3~\cite{jiang2023mistral}, KL's downstream advantage is consistent: HellaSwag +8.8pp, ARC +9.0pp, WG +5.6pp vs.\ TEAL at 30\%, with PPL 6.77 vs.\ 8.38; the 50\% capacity cliff also holds cross-architecture (MMLU 26.4\%$\approx$random; Appendix~\ref{app:cross_arch}). % exp_id: mistral_kl30_downstream, mistral_teal30_downstream, mistral_teal50_downstream
On Qwen-2.5-14B~\cite{qwen2024qwen25}, KL's PPL advantage generalizes at 30\% ($1.47\times$ dense) but MMLU falls to 63.7\% vs.\ TEAL's 71.8\% ($-$8.1pp; Appendix~\ref{app:14b}); this reversal is absent at 7B on both architectures, suggesting scale-dependent effects. % exp_id: qwen14b_kl30_s42, qwen14b_teal30_eval, qwen14b_bce30_s42_eval
SPON~\cite{xu2026spon} bias correction is an optional, marginal component (2.4\% PPL improvement; Appendix~\ref{app:spon}). % exp_id: spon_2048_s42

\paragraph{Inference Efficiency.}
\label{sec:overhead}
The predictor adds 3.17\,ms (2.7\% overhead; 0.099\,ms/layer vs.\ 2.43\,ms for the dense FFN) using 75.96M params (0.9\% of base), while eliminating BCE's 67.2M compensation network.
Actual speedup requires sparse kernel integration (Appendix~\ref{app:latency}). % exp_id: latency_profiling

